WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.270
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.200
Reinforcement learning is a technique in the
field of machine learning to learn how to

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:16.289
navigate in a labyrinth, play a video game,
or to teach a digital creature to walk. Usually,

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:22.259
we are interested in a series of actions that
are in some sense, optimal in a given environment.

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:28.249
Despite the fact that many enormous tomes
exist to discuss the mathematical details,

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:34.010
the intuition behind the algorithm itself
is incredibly simple. Choose an action, and

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:39.129
if you get rewarded for it, keep doing it.
If the rewards are not coming, try something

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:45.170
else. The reward can be, for instance, our
score in a computer game or how far our digital

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.890
creature could walk.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:52.079
It is usually quite difficult to learn things
where the reward comes long after our action

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:56.809
because we don't know when exactly the point
was when we did something well. This is one

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:02.280
of the reasons why Google DeepMind will try
to conquer strategy games in the future, because

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:08.070
this is a genre where good plays usually include
long-term planning that reinforcement learning

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.860
techniques don't really excel at. By the way,
this just in: they the have just published

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:18.270
an excellent paper on including curiosity
in this equation in a way that helps long

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.030
term planning remarkably.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.530
As more techniques pop up in this direction,
it is getting abundantly clear that we need

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.850
a framework where they can undergo stringent
testing. This means that the amount of collected

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:35.990
rewards and scores should be computed the
same way, and in the same physical framework.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:42.140
OpenAI is a non-profit company boasting an
impressive roster of top-tier researchers

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:48.030
who embarked on the quest to develop open
and ethical artificial intelligence techniques.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.950
We've had a previous episode on this when
the company was freshly founded, and as you

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.400
might have guessed, the link is available
in the description box.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.940
They have recently published their first major
project that goes by the name Gym. Gym is

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.719
a unified framework that puts reinforcement
learning techniques on an equal footing. Anyone

00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:14.890
can submit their solutions which are run on
the same problems, and as a nice bit of gamification,

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:20.189
leaderboards are established to see which
technique emerges victorious. These environments

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:25.760
range from a variety of computer games to
different balancing tasks. Some simpler reference

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.459
solutions are also provided for many of them
as a starting point. This place is like Disneyworld

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:35.400
for someone who is excited about the the field
of reinforcement learning.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.019
With more and more techniques, the subfield
gets more saturated, it gets more and more

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:45.269
difficult to be the first at something. That's
a great challenge for researchers. From a

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:51.249
consumer point of view, this means that better
techniques will pop up day by day. And, as

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:56.060
I like to say quite often, we have really
exciting times ahead of us.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:01.889
A quick shoutout to Experiment, a startup
to help research projects come to fruition

00:03:01.889 --> 00:03:03.859
by crowdsourcing them.

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:10.060
Current experiments include really cool projects
like how we could implement better anti-doping

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:17.060
policies for professional sports, or to show
on a computer screen how our visual imagination

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.159
works.

00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:22.619
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

